  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Digital technology adoption is rapidly changing the interaction and work patterns of Ethiopians, connecting communities and workers in increasingly sophisticated ways, could potentially unlock new opportunities. In almost every sector, technological changes are shortening the lifespan of workers&#x2019; skills. Thus, better training and lifelong learning are essential to ensure that the workforce and its employers can cope with the upheavals of Ethiopia&#x2019;s Fourth Industrial Revolution. This involves moving from &#x201c;education for employment&#x201d; to &#x201c;education for employability&#x201d; and from &#x201c;job security&#x201d; to &#x201c;career security.&#x201d; 
In view of these rapid changes, CETU came up with a plan to develop and implement a comprehensive policy alternative aimed at contributing to the resolution of the youth unemployment crisis and low youth union density. The CPO intends to support this initiative by CETU to come up with alternative policy document envisaged to respond to the youth unemployment crisis and low youth union density which will be presented for negotiations at policy development decision-making forums. Besides stakeholders including public authorities, the private sector, pro-union civil society organizations and others encouraged to illuminate their arguments in public debates on the anticipated pro-youth employment architecture, particularly during tripartite social dialogue and collective bargaining initiatives. The ILO focuses on providing technical support to CETU and share experience from other countries on how to craft the policy document that addresses the current and future challenges posed by the digital transformations.</narrative>
  </description>
